INTRODUCTION \
\
\
\'93What makes an apparently ordinary human being, \
unfavoured by cither heredity or environment, sud- \
denly achieve greatness?\'94 Here is a question which \
has occupied the attention of many an eminent mind, \
and has elicited a variety of answers. A more fasci- \
nating question seldom asked is, \'93what makes a great \
man, tutored from birth in the ways of greatness, re- \
linquish all his claims at the climax of his career to \
return to ordinary living?\'94 \
\
The present volume is a study of this rare pheno- \
menon as reflected m the life and thought of one njan \
who had greatness thnist upon him early in Me and \
who later achieved a different kind of greatness, only \
to give it all up: J. Krishnamurti. It is ^a ^trangd \
story, now almost forgotten largely due to KrisKna- \
murti\'92s own efforts. And it uncovers a strange* miitd, \
still very imperfectly understood. \
\
It should be stressed at the very outset that, by \
Krishnamurti\'92s own admission, there is nothing \'91super- \
natural\'92 about this mind; nothing belongs to it that \
does not or cannot bel mg to the mind of every' other \
human being sufficiently aware of his condition. We \
hope to open that mind to a wider audience. \
\
There are several volumes of Krishnamurti\'92s writings \
available. To those acquainted with them and to those \
who have attended Krishnamurti\'92s talks, this volume \
may serve as a refresher course. Krishnamurti\'92s think- \
ing is too elusive for those unaccustomed to logical \
reasoning as well as for those obsessed by it. For the \
former it is too abstruse. For the latter it is perhaps \
