Chapter I \
\
\
J. Krishnamurti: The Man and His Mind \
\
by LUIS S R. VAS \
\
\
The quest for self-discovery is forever new. From \
Socrates to Sartre, from Samkara of India to vSuzuki \
of Japan, both East and West have produced thinkers \
who have contributed to a greater or lesser extent to \
the understanding of man\'92s condition here (m eartli. \
\
In our time this quest is divided between philosophy, \
psychology and the social sciences, and many thinkers \
have stretched their thouglit to cover all thre<?, while \
others have departed ccanplctcly frean the beaten track \
to search in regions yet unexplored. To the latter \
category belongs J. Krishnamurti. \
\
As a renowned \'91non-Guru\'92, Kri.'.hnamurti differs \
fundamentally from most philosophers in tliat he has \
resisted the tendency to weave his insights into a sys- \
tem, though they have much in common with such \
schools as the existentialist and the Zen. \
\
Jiddu K^i^h^amurti^s career is an extraordinary' one \
by any standard and is of considerable interest to \
students of human nature and, of course, of Krishna- \
murti\'92s mind. He was born in 18*^5, (or thereabouts, \
for the records are not very reliable) in a large South \
Indian family of strict, if poor. Brahmins. At the age \
of 12 he was adopted along with his youngest brother \
Nityanand by Mrs Annie Bcsanl who had an uncanny \
knack for \'91discovering\'92 and encouraging talented minds: \
among her finds can be counted B. Shiva Rao. Sri \
Prakash, V. K. Krishna Mcnon and Rukmmi Devi. \
\
\
\
