\
\
But when he happened to take a few steps to the \
left, they sat up and obviously paid close attention \
t() the lecture. In short they conditioned Minsky \
by repeatedly punishing him for moving in one dir- \
ection and rewarding him for moving in the oppo- \
site direction. Within half an hcuir he slopped pac- \
ing altogether and stood like a cigar store Indian \
near the lefthand edge of the blackboard. So subtly \
had he been habituated that he did not realise an \
experiment was in progress, and that he was the \
guinea pig- ironically, since Minsky is a leading au- \
thority on the theory of automatons.\'94 \
\
\
Habit, then, is a conditioning factor too. It pemits \
us to view everything as a routine, as a repetition of \
what happened the previous day and is expected to \
happen the next day, and saves us the trouble of think- \
ing anew, from \'91moment to moment'. \
\
Consequently we are only partly conscious of what \
affects us and react to it automatically, living a life of \
somnolence, doing what the interplay of emotions and \
habits will have us do. \
\
In his book. \'93TOC ACT OF CRFATION\'94, Arthur \
Koestlcr says, \'93There are two ways of escaping our \
more or less automatised routines of thinking and be- \
having. Tlie first, of course, is the plunge in dreaming \
\
and dreamlike states TTic other is also an escape \
\
IYmiti boredom, intellectual predicaments and emotional \
frastrations\'97 but in the opposite direction, it is sig- \
j'.alled by the .spontaneous flash of insight which sIk'ws \
a familiar situation in a new light and elicits a \
response to it. . . .it makes understand what it is to be \
awake, to be living on several planes at once \'94 \
\
Koestler\'92s book is, among other things, an attempt \
to formulate the theoretical foundation of this pheno- \
memin: But it gives no more than an inkling of how \
this \'91escape\'92 can be effected, on how the \'91flash of in- \
sight\'92 can be made to occur. On the other hand this \
\
