THE MIND OF J. KRISHNAMURTI \
\
\
is precisely what Krishnamurti seeks to do, so as to \
enable us to live a life free from psychological condi- \
tioning by acquiring a \'91sensitivity\'92, an \'91awareness\'92. \
\
Life being in a state of movement, so he argues, we \
must not regard any of our relationships from a fixed \
standpoint. \'93We must be willing to accept change \
in others and be willing to change ourselves\'92\'92. This \
willingness must be, not merely an intellectual disposi- \
tion to change, but one involving our whole persona- \
lity \'97 we must fee/ this change continually. \
\
And how does one get to feel the change? In Krish- \
namurti\'92s words, \'93by being what you really are. By \
trying to see what is. You see, sir, (somehow) I \
have acquired a certain character. Now I must try \
and see myself as I am and I must make no clTort to \
he anything else. ...lam not advocating self-indulgence, \
that a thief remain a thief. I must not submit to my \
weakness but I should not indulge in the opposite of \
my weakness either, as a way of geting rid of it .... \'92\'92 \
\
I..et us imagine that we are at a given moment, en- \
vious or bor^. These emotions are painful. We do \
not like them and so we try to avoid tliem. This \
creates a conflict between what we are and what we \
want to DC, between envy and non-envy, between bore- \
dom and excitement. Tlie result is that we do not get \
away from envy and grow more and more displeased \
with ourselves. If, on the other hand, we observe this \
envy in a passive and dispassionate way with a recep- \
tive mind determined to examine the phenomenon with- \
out passing judgement, without condemnation, look- \
ing for its causes with our total attention, but never \
trying to push it away, then there is no conflict and \
the envy is apprehended from an entirely new angle: \
\'93Suddenly I will discover that a transformation tikes \
place in myself without any planning on my part, a \
creative transformation. My sensitivity has come into \
play.\'94 Radically transform^ envy now has ceased to \
have its original form. It is no longer envy and we no \
