One would argue that even tiiis realm is, on the level \
of \'91becoming\'92- But it can not be a \'91becoming\'92, for it \
is not an approximation to, an ideal. In the state of \
\'91pure being\'92 itself there may be such a thing as pure \
and spontaneous becoming, a creative, unfoldment of \
life, a \'91deathless renewal\'92 in the eternal fulness of the \
present moment. \
\
Krishnamurti, apostle as he is of complete spon- \
taneity, asserts repeatedly and forcibly, that no deli- \
berate intervention can put an end to the cycle of con- \
ditioning, the cycle of self-consciousness and of sorrow. \
The reason he gives is simple: the desire to break the \
cycle springs from the cycle itself, from the very con- \
ditioning it wants to break; it is ultimately a desire to \
gain or attain something else and such desire is the \
very substance of the \'91I\'92. Such desire can bring about \
